Falsifies `kernel::virtio::tests::the_iommu_faults_a_dma_that_escapes_the_domain` on AMD-Vi.

Architecture: x86_64
Environment: NIFE_IOMMU=amd

The confined device's device table entry stops translating. `AmdVi::device_table_entry` (in
`crates/paging` since provisional milestone 767 (AMD-Vi hardening before the first AMD boot),
moved there so Kani could prove it) drops `Mode` = 4
and keeps `V`, `TV`, `IR` and `IW`, which is the entry AMD 48882 rev 2.62, Table 7 defines as
"translation disabled, access controlled by IR and IW": the device reaches every address with read
and write permission, and the page table the seam built for it is never walked. QEMU 11.1.1 treats
the same entry as pass-through (`enable_nodma_mode`), so both agree on what this does. It is the
one-field difference between a confined device and an unconfined one on this hardware, which is
the whole of AMD-Vi's confinement claim, and the record is AMD-Vi's alone: it needs the AMD-Vi
machine (`Environment:`), and VT-d's evidence for this test is the VT-d unit's own.

Expected to fail: the `fault.expect("the device read an out-of-domain address and the IOMMU recorded
no fault ...")` in the test. The escape read of the available ring lands, so no fault is logged for
it; nothing the device does afterwards is refused either, because nothing is translated.
